The {{nihongo|'''Sanese Official Portrait'''|三國公式肖像画画|Sangoku Kōshiki Shōzōga}}, or {{nihongo|'''Sanese Official Photograph'''|三國公式写真|Sangoku Kōshiki Shashin}} for modern photography is a highly distinctive and formalized style of portraiture used for government officials, military leaders, and corporate executives in [[Okaiken]]. 

Characterized by a hyper-realistic yet distinctly "painterly" aesthetic, the style is deeply ingrained in the political and corporate culture of Okaiken. It is most famous for its aesthetic resemblance to the character portraits found in the popular Okaiken grand strategy video game franchise, ''Terumasa's Ambition''. The style is designed to project unwavering resolve, formidable competence, and martial-like authority.

== Visual Characteristics ==
The Sanese portrait style adheres to a strict set of visual conventions that bridge the gap between traditional oil painting and modern digital photography:

* '''The Painterly Finish:''' Modern portraits are heavily retouched using digital painting techniques to resemble high-end, smooth oil paintings. Skin textures, lighting, and fabrics are smoothed and stylized to give the subject a larger-than-life, almost illustrated appearance. In other instances, the photos are submitted to an official painter, where they recompose the image into a real acrylic or oil painting on canvas.
* '''Dramatic Lighting:''' Subjects are typically lit with strong, directional lighting that emphasizes facial structure, deepening shadows around the eyes and jawline to project intensity and wisdom.
* '''The Stance:''' The subject is almost always posed in a three-quarter or forward-facing stance. For modern officials, the arms are frequently crossed over the chest, a posture meant to convey defensive strength and unyielding confidence. 
* '''The Gaze:''' The subject makes direct, unblinking eye contact with the lens/viewer. Smiles are strictly avoided; instead, a stern, serious, or deeply contemplative expression is required.

===Base Prompt===

A hyper-realistic digital painting in the Nobunaga's Ambition Portrait style of a [AGE]-year-old [GENDER] of Japanese descent. Subject has [HAIR/FACIAL FEATURES] and wears [CLOTHING/ATTIRE]. Posed in a [FORWARD-FACING / 3/4] stance with arms crossed. Stern, unsmiling expression making direct eye contact. Art style mimics smooth, heavy oil paintings from grand strategy games like Nobunaga's Ambition. Dramatic directional lighting, plain neutral grey studio gradient background.

== History and Evolution ==
=== Imperial Era Origins ===
The roots of the Sanese style can be traced back to the late 19th and early 20th centuries, when Okaiken began modernizing its military and government. Early portraits of state leaders, such as the portrait of [[Juichi Miyazaki]], utilized heavy oil paints to depict subjects in full military regalia. These early paintings established the cultural expectation that an official must look stern, heavily decorated, and formidable, with an emphasis on rich, dark colors and intense facial expressions.

=== Feudal Inspiration and ''Terumasa's Ambition'' ===
The modern iteration of the style was heavily influenced by pop culture—specifically, the artwork depicting historical warlords from Okaiken's feudal period. In the late 20th century, the release of the strategy game ''Terumasa's Ambition'' popularized a highly stylized, intense, and painterly way of drawing historical figures. 

Politicians and public relations firms in the capital of [[Okami]] realized that this specific art style resonated deeply with the Okaiken public, evoking a sense of strong, historical leadership. By the early 2000s, state photographers began employing digital artists to retouch official government photographs to mimic the ''Terumasa's Ambition'' aesthetic. 

=== Modern Usage ===
Today, almost all cabinet members, high-ranking officers in the Okaiken Armed Forces, and CEOs of major Sanese conglomerates utilize this style for their official press releases and [[Main Page|TALOD Wiki]] pages. 

The modern Sanese Official Portrait typically features the subject in a sharply tailored, dark business suit with a solid or subtly patterned tie. Despite the modern attire, the digital processing ensures they carry the same visual weight as a feudal lord in armor or a 19th-century general. The crossed-arms motif has become so standard that political commentators in Okaiken often analyze the tightness of a politician's folded arms to gauge their political positioning.

